The archive was created and then verified two ways: it opens as a valid ZIP, and its entry count of 18,402 matches the source exactly. That check matters because Compress-Archive can complete while silently skipping locked files.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem 'D:\Projects\2023-client-work' -Recurse -File | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,2)}}
- Get-Volume -DriveLetter D | Select-Object @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.SizeRemaining/1GB)}}
- Compress-Archive -Path 'D:\Projects\2023-client-work\*' -DestinationPath 'D:\Archive\2023-client-work.zip' -CompressionLevel Optimal
Recommendation: Keep the original until you have opened a few files from the archive. Note Compress-Archive struggles above about 2 GB of source data on older PowerShell versions - if you archive larger sets, 7-Zip is more reliable.

C: is at 2.5% free. Three things account for most of it: 80 GB of user media and a VM disk, 68 GB of unbounded shadow copy storage, and 20 GB of hibernation plus page file. The shadow storage is the least obvious and the safest to reclaim.
Commands used:
- Get-Volume -DriveLetter C | Select-Object Size, SizeRemaining, @{N='PctFree';E={[math]::Round($_.SizeRemaining/$_.Size*100,1)}}
- Get-ChildItem C:\ -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object Length -gt 1GB | Sort-Object Length -Descending | Select-Object -First 6 FullName, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Length/1GB,2)}}
- vssadmin list shadowstorage
Recommendation: Capping shadow storage to 20 GB reclaims about 48 GB immediately without touching your files. The 42 GB video and 38 GB VM disk are yours to decide on - I would move rather than delete those. Tell me which to act on and I will show a plan first.

