Event Viewer logs were explicitly excluded, since those are the audit and diagnostic history rather than disposable text files. The 90-day floor keeps recent CBS logs, which are what you need if a Windows Update failure has to be diagnosed.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Windows\Logs' -Recurse -File -Include '*.log','*.etl' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-90) | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,2)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\ProgramData' -Recurse -File -Include '*.log' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-90) | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,2)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Windows\Logs','C:\ProgramData' -Recurse -File -Include '*.log','*.etl' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-90) | Remove-Item -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Recommendation: This is worth repeating a couple of times a year, not monthly - logs older than 90 days are rarely useful, but logs from last month often are. If one application is generating gigabytes of logs, its logging level is the real problem.

A dry run was performed first, showing 4,096 files totalling 3.2 GB currently match the deletion criteria. Only after that concrete number was presented and confirmed was the recurring task created.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem $env:TEMP -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-7) | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.Sum/1MB)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Windows\Temp' -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-7) | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='MB';E={[int]($_.Sum/1MB)}}
- $a=New-ScheduledTaskAction -Execute 'powershell.exe' -Argument '-NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command "Get-ChildItem $env:TEMP,C:\Windows\Temp -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-7) | Remove-Item -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"'; $t=New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Weekly -DaysOfWeek Sunday -At 23:00; Register-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'Chanakya-WeeklyTempCleanup' -Action $a -Trigger $t -RunLevel Highest
Recommendation: The 7-day age threshold protects files a running installer may still need. Never point this at broader paths like Downloads or a user profile root - temp directories are safe because their contents are disposable by definition.

D: currently holds 84 GB of backups against 1.6 TB free, so there is no immediate problem - but a mirrored backup grows silently with the source, and a full destination causes backups to fail quietly. The monitor warns rather than deleting anything.
Commands used:
- Get-Volume -DriveLetter D | Select-Object Size, SizeRemaining, @{N='PctFree';E={[math]::Round($_.SizeRemaining/$_.Size*100,1)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'D:\Backups' -Recurse -File | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
- $a=New-ScheduledTaskAction -Execute 'powershell.exe' -Argument '-NoProfile -WindowStyle Hidden -Command "$v=Get-Volume -DriveLetter D; if(($v.SizeRemaining/$v.Size) -lt 0.15){Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Windows.Forms; $n=New-Object System.Windows.Forms.NotifyIcon; $n.Icon=[System.Drawing.SystemIcons]::Warning; $n.Visible=$true; $n.ShowBalloonTip(30000,''Backup drive filling up'',\"D: has $([math]::Round($v.SizeRemaining/1GB,1)) GB free\",''Warning''); Start-Sleep 35}"'; $t=New-ScheduledTaskTrigger -Daily -At 09:00; Register-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'Chanakya-BackupDriveMonitor' -Action $a -Trigger $t
Recommendation: Do not let a monitor auto-delete old backups. If space does get tight, decide deliberately what retention you want - automatic pruning triggered by a threshold is how people lose the one backup they needed.

62.4 GB was copied with robocopy reporting zero failures, and both the file count and total byte size match the source exactly. The originals remain on C: deliberately - copy and delete were kept as separate decisions rather than a single move.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Users\jdoe\Pictures' -Recurse -File | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
- Get-Volume -DriveLetter E | Select-Object FileSystem, @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.SizeRemaining/1GB)}}
- robocopy 'C:\Users\jdoe\Pictures' 'E:\Photos' /E /R:2 /W:5 /NFL /NDL
Recommendation: Open a handful of photos from E: before deleting anything, especially older ones. External drives fail, so this should be a second copy rather than your only copy - if these are irreplaceable, keep a cloud or second-drive copy too.

