- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Users\jdoe\Pictures' -Recurse -File | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
- Get-Volume -DriveLetter E | Select-Object FileSystem, @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.SizeRemaining/1GB)}}
- robocopy 'C:\Users\jdoe\Pictures' 'E:\Photos' /E /R:2 /W:5 /NFL /NDL
Recommendation: Open a handful of photos from E: before deleting anything, especially older ones. External drives fail, so this should be a second copy rather than your only copy - if these are irreplaceable, keep a cloud or second-drive copy too.

C: is at 2.5% free. Three things account for most of it: 80 GB of user media and a VM disk, 68 GB of unbounded shadow copy storage, and 20 GB of hibernation plus page file. The shadow storage is the least obvious and the safest to reclaim.
Commands used:
- Get-Volume -DriveLetter C | Select-Object Size, SizeRemaining, @{N='PctFree';E={[math]::Round($_.SizeRemaining/$_.Size*100,1)}}
- Get-ChildItem C:\ -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object Length -gt 1GB | Sort-Object Length -Descending | Select-Object -First 6 FullName, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Length/1GB,2)}}
- vssadmin list shadowstorage
Recommendation: Capping shadow storage to 20 GB reclaims about 48 GB immediately without touching your files. The 42 GB video and 38 GB VM disk are yours to decide on - I would move rather than delete those. Tell me which to act on and I will show a plan first.

Event Viewer logs were explicitly excluded, since those are the audit and diagnostic history rather than disposable text files. The 90-day floor keeps recent CBS logs, which are what you need if a Windows Update failure has to be diagnosed.
Commands used:
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Windows\Logs' -Recurse -File -Include '*.log','*.etl' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-90) | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,2)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\ProgramData' -Recurse -File -Include '*.log' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-90) | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,2)}}
- Get-ChildItem 'C:\Windows\Logs','C:\ProgramData' -Recurse -File -Include '*.log','*.etl' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object LastWriteTime -lt (Get-Date).AddDays(-90) | Remove-Item -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Recommendation: This is worth repeating a couple of times a year, not monthly - logs older than 90 days are rarely useful, but logs from last month often are. If one application is generating gigabytes of logs, its logging level is the real problem.

14 video files over 1 GB account for 78.6 GB, and the largest single item - a 42 GB raw wedding video from 2023 - sits on the drive that is nearly full while D: has 412 GB free. Moving that one file alone more than triples your free space on C:.
Commands used:
- Get-Volume | Where-Object DriveType -eq 'Fixed' | Select-Object DriveLetter, @{N='FreeGB';E={[int]($_.SizeRemaining/1GB)}}
- Get-ChildItem C:\,D:\ -Include '*.mp4','*.mkv','*.mov','*.avi' -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object Length -gt 1GB | Sort-Object Length -Descending | Select-Object -First 5 FullName, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Length/1GB,1)}}, LastWriteTime
- Get-ChildItem C:\,D:\ -Include '*.mp4','*.mkv','*.mov','*.avi' -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object Length -gt 1GB | Measure-Object Length -Sum | Select-Object Count, @{N='GB';E={[math]::Round($_.Sum/1GB,1)}}
Recommendation: Moving rather than deleting is the obvious step for the wedding footage. The 8.4 GB screen recording on the Desktop is worth reviewing - screen recordings are rarely kept deliberately. Tell me which to move and I will copy, verify, then remove.
